package api

// Explain intent separately from the wire catalog: an encoder name alone does
// not tell an integrator whether a command belongs in their application.
var commandDescriptions = map[string]string{
	"LedCtrl":                        "Turn a printer light on or off. Use this for a chamber/work-light switch in your app; the wrapper supplies the protocol's timing fields. The low-level command also supports firmware-specific light modes such as flashing.",
	"SetFan":                         "Set a controllable fan's speed. Use this for part-cooling, auxiliary or chamber fan controls; percentages are 0–100, not a 0–255 PWM value. Available fans depend on the printer model.",
	"SetBedTemp":                     "Set the bed's target temperature. Use this to preheat the bed or send zero to turn bed heating off; the printer enforces its own thermal limits.",
	"SetNozzleTemp":                  "Set a nozzle's target temperature. Use this to preheat the selected extruder or send zero to turn heating off; this does not load filament or start printing.",
	"PrintSpeed":                     "Choose a print speed mode. Use this to switch a running job between silent, standard, sport and ludicrous rather than rewriting its G-code.",
	"Pause":                          "Pause the current print. Use this when the user wants to interrupt a job temporarily and resume it later; it is not a cancellation.",
	"Resume":                         "Resume a paused print. Use this after the reason for pausing has been addressed; the printer can reject it if the job or error state does not permit resuming.",
	"Stop":                           "Cancel the current print. Use this only when the user wants to abandon the job, not for a temporary pause; do not assume a stopped job can be resumed.",
	"GcodeFile":                      "Start printing a file already stored on the printer. Use this for local printer-storage files; it does not upload a file. The resource route defaults bed leveling, flow calibration, vibration calibration and first-layer inspection to enabled.",
	"ProjectFile":                    "Start a project-based print using Bambu's project parameters. This advanced alternative to a simple stored-file print carries project URLs, plate selection, identifiers, AMS mapping and other job options. Its body is passed through; obtain valid project metadata before using it.",
	"SkipObjects":                    "Skip selected objects in the current print. Use this to abandon failed objects while allowing the rest of a multi-object job to continue; IDs must come from that job's object metadata.",
	"PrintOption":                    "Change firmware print options. This exposes Handy's free-form print_option message for advanced settings not represented by the simple job controls; supported fields vary by firmware, so consult the printer's protocol before sending arbitrary keys.",
	"TaskLockErase":                  "Send the firmware's task-lock erase command. It exists for the app's task-lock workflow, not routine print cancellation. The exact lock state and side effects are not established here; use job stop for normal cancellation.",
	"Ignore":                         "Acknowledge a print error using its error and job identifiers. This exposes the app's error-recovery workflow; use it only after understanding and addressing the fault, not to suppress unknown printer errors.",
	"CleanPrintError":                "Clear a reported print error. Use this when acknowledging a fault that has been resolved, supplying the relevant subtask/error information when required by firmware; it does not repair the underlying problem.",
	"IdleIgnore":                     "Send the app's idle-error acknowledgment. This advanced operation distinguishes errors outside an active print; the numeric type/error meanings are firmware-defined and are not enumerated by this wrapper.",
	"UiopClosePrintError":            "Dismiss a printer error dialog through the app's UI-operation command. This closes the notification rather than physically fixing the printer; use it only for a known, resolved error.",
	"MoveAxis":                       "Jog selected axes by relative distances in millimetres. Use this for manual positioning, with an optional positive feed rate in mm/min. This moves real hardware; check clearance and printer state before use.",
	"HomeAxes":                       "Home selected printer axes. Use this to establish their reference positions before manual work; an empty body on the resource route selects all axes. Homing moves real hardware, so ensure the printer is clear.",
	"BackToCenter":                   "Request the printer's return-to-center movement. This exposes a firmware positioning action used by Handy; exact movement and model support are firmware-defined, so it is not a substitute for an explicit relative jog.",
	"MoveToCapture":                  "Move to the firmware's capture position. This supports the app's image-capture workflow and physically repositions the toolhead; it does not return a camera image or video stream.",
	"XyzCtrl":                        "Send the firmware's axis/direction/mode control message. This is the app's alternate motion protocol; most integrations should use motion/move with explicit distances instead of relying on undocumented numeric modes.",
	"GcodeLine":                      "Send raw G-code to the printer. This escape hatch is for operations not covered by a higher-level resource; it can move axes, heat components or affect a running job, so prefer the named controls when possible.",
	"AmsSelectSlot":                  "Select an AMS filament slot. Use this for the app's slot-selection workflow; selection is distinct from commanding the complete filament load/change sequence.",
	"AmsChangeFilament":              "Request an AMS filament change or load. Use this when switching the printer to a particular tray; slot addressing and optional temperatures/extruder selection follow the command catalog. This can move filament and trigger firmware-controlled heating.",
	"UnloadFilament":                 "Request the firmware's filament-unload sequence. Use this to remove the loaded filament before changing material or servicing the feed path; the printer controls the sequence and may require a suitable temperature/state.",
	"AmsFilamentSetting":             "Update the material metadata assigned to an AMS tray. Use this to set material type, color, preset and nozzle-temperature information; it describes the spool rather than starting a load or setting the current heater target.",
	"AmsUserSetting":                 "Configure AMS spool-reading and remaining-filament options. Use this for persistent feeder preferences, such as reading trays at startup, rather than commanding an immediate filament change.",
	"AmsControl":                     "Send an AMS workflow control action. Use this for recovery actions such as retry, pause, resume or done when the feeder is waiting for intervention; only send actions appropriate to its current state.",
	"AmsGetRfid":                     "Ask the AMS to read a tray's RFID information. Use this after inserting or changing a tagged spool when you want the feeder to refresh its material identification.",
	"AmsReset":                       "Request an AMS reset. This is a recovery/maintenance operation, not a way to refresh your UI; use printer state for reads and avoid resetting a feeder in use.",
	"AutoAmsBind":                    "Request automatic AMS binding. This exposes the app's feeder-association workflow for supported hardware; it is not cloud account binding and is usually unnecessary for an already configured AMS.",
	"AmsFilamentDrying":              "Start or configure filament drying on a supported AMS. Use this for an explicit drying workflow with material, temperature, duration and other required settings; it can heat the drying hardware and is not supported by every AMS.",
	"AutoStopAmsDry":                 "Request the firmware's AMS drying stop action. Use this to end drying on supported hardware; it does not stop a print job.",
	"CameraCapturePicSet":            "Enable or disable the camera's picture-capture feature. This changes a camera setting; it does not return an image in the HTTP response.",
	"CameraRecordSet":                "Enable or disable camera recording. Use this to control the printer's recording setting, not to fetch a recording or open a live video stream.",
	"CameraTimelapse":                "Enable or disable timelapse capture. Use this to choose whether supported camera hardware records print progress for a timelapse; this route does not download the resulting video.",
	"CameraResolutionSet":            "Set the printer camera's resolution. Use this when configuring supported recording/camera modes; accepted resolution strings are firmware-specific.",
	"CameraResolutionGet":            "Ask the camera to report its resolution. This sends a query over MQTT because the value is owned by the printer, rather than reading a stored HTTP resource.",
	"CameraDeleteOldestTimelapse":    "Request deletion of the oldest timelapse on the specified storage. This is a destructive storage-management action; it exists to support the app's recording-space workflow, not to disable future timelapses.",
	"CameraGetMediaInfo":             "Query camera media information. Use this to request the firmware's recording/storage metadata using its sub_command selector; the wrapper does not interpret every selector or serve media files itself.",
	"LiveviewPrepare":                "Prepare the printer side of a live-view session. This sends Bambu/TUTK connection credentials used by the app's camera handshake. It is an advanced protocol step, not a video endpoint; streaming transport is not implemented by this wrapper.",
	"XcamControlSet":                 "Configure a vision/detection module. Use this for supported camera-based print monitoring and its halt/skip behavior; module names and available detection capabilities vary by model and firmware.",
	"GetHeightFromXcam":              "Request the vision system's height information. This exposes a specialized camera/measurement query for supported hardware; it is not the current print layer count.",
	"GetVersion":                     "Ask the printer to report firmware/component versions. Use this when showing device software information or diagnosing compatibility; it does not install an update.",
	"UpgradeStart":                   "Start a firmware update for a specified component. This is a maintenance operation requiring the correct module, version and firmware URL; incorrect inputs can disrupt the printer. Do not use it during a print or as an automatic retry.",
	"UpgradeConfirm":                 "Confirm a firmware upgrade in the app's update workflow. Use this only when that workflow is awaiting confirmation; it is not a general health check or a way to initiate an update by itself.",
	"UpgradeConsistencyConfirm":      "Confirm a firmware consistency update. This supports the printer's component-version reconciliation workflow; only send it when you understand the pending update state.",
	"UpgradeGetHistory":              "Ask the printer for its firmware update history. Use this to inspect earlier update information while troubleshooting, without initiating an upgrade.",
	"UpgradeHistory":                 "Send the app's firmware-history selection parameters. This advanced operation carries firmware_optional data; its precise selection/side effects are not fully established. Use firmware/history/query for an ordinary history request.",
	"McForAmsFirmwareUpgrade":        "Request the AMS firmware-upgrade coordination step. This targets an AMS identifier as part of the app's update workflow; it is not a normal feeder control and should not be sent during printing or filament handling.",
	"Calibration":                    "Start printer calibration routines selected by the firmware option bitmask. Use this for deliberate maintenance such as bed leveling or vibration calibration, not to read existing calibration results; it can move and heat the printer.",
	"ExtrusionCaliGet":               "Ask for extrusion-calibration information. Use this to inspect firmware calibration data rather than starting a calibration or extruding filament.",
	"SetPtcStep":                     "Advance a step in the app's PTC calibration workflow. This is an advanced step/offset/rotation protocol; the exact workflow and safe parameter meanings are not established here, so do not treat it as ordinary printer calibration.",
	"HolderCali":                     "Request nozzle-holder calibration on supported hardware. This exists for the app's nozzle-holder maintenance workflow; model support and resulting movement are firmware-dependent.",
	"SelectExtruder":                 "Select an extruder by index. Use this on supported multi-extruder hardware when choosing the active extruder; this is distinct from setting its temperature or loading an AMS spool.",
	"SetExtrusionLength":             "Set the firmware's extrusion-length parameter for an extruder. This is an advanced extrusion-control setting; the catalog alone does not establish whether a particular firmware only stores it or also performs motion.",
	"GetExtruder":                    "Ask the printer for information about an extruder. Use this to inspect supported extruder state/configuration rather than changing the active extruder.",
	"SetNozzle":                      "Update a nozzle's recorded type, wear and diameter information. Use this after replacing/configuring compatible nozzle hardware; it describes the nozzle, not its heater target.",
	"RefreshNozzle":                  "Request a refresh of nozzle information. Use this when the app needs the firmware to re-report nozzle metadata after hardware changes, rather than guessing the currently installed nozzle.",
	"NozzleInfoConfirm":              "Confirm nozzle information in the app's nozzle workflow. This acknowledges configuration information; it is usually unnecessary unless the printer is specifically waiting for that confirmation.",
	"NozzleExchange":                 "Request a nozzle exchange between the specified positions. This is a physical maintenance action for compatible hardware, not a metadata update; verify model support and clearance before use.",
	"HolderNozzleRefresh":            "Refresh information for a nozzle in a specified holder. Use this when managing compatible nozzle-holder hardware; ordinary single-nozzle printers may not support it.",
	"GetAutoNozzleMapping":           "Request an automatic filament-to-nozzle mapping from the firmware. This advanced planning query requires filament, AMS and nozzle metadata; it is for preparing compatible multi-material/multi-nozzle jobs, not an automatic print-start command.",
	"GetNozzleTemp":                  "Ask the firmware to report nozzle-temperature information. This is a low-level query; most integrations can read the merged printer state instead of issuing a separate command.",
	"SetAgainstContinuedHeatingMode": "Configure the firmware's continued-heating protection mode. This changes a heating-related protection setting, not the heater target; its exact firmware-specific behavior is not established, so leave it alone unless implementing that known workflow.",
	"SetCtt":                         "Set the firmware's CTT value. This exists for protocol completeness; the catalog gives an integer ctt_val but does not establish its units or safe operating meaning. Use the named bed/nozzle temperature routes for ordinary heating.",
	"BuzzerCtrl":                     "Enable or disable the printer buzzer setting. Use this for an audible-alert preference on supported firmware; this is not a request to play an arbitrary sound.",
	"FireExtCtrl":                    "Send the firmware's fire-extinguisher control command. This safety-related protocol is exposed for completeness, but the numeric command meanings and model support are not established here. Do not use it as a routine printer action.",
	"SetAirduct":                     "Choose an airduct mode and submode. This configures supported airflow-routing hardware rather than simply setting fan speed; the numeric modes are firmware-specific.",
	"GetAirductCfg":                  "Ask for the firmware's airduct configuration. Use this to inspect supported airflow routing before changing its mode.",
	"PartControlAps":                 "Send the app's APS part-control action. This is a specialized hardware protocol; the catalog does not fully establish supported actions or their effects, so prefer dedicated fan, motion and heating controls for normal use.",
	"GetFan":                         "Ask the firmware to report fan information. Use this for low-level fan diagnostics; the merged printer state is usually sufficient for a status display.",
	"Refill":                         "Request the app's filament-refill action. This is a firmware-specific feeder workflow, distinct from editing spool metadata; only use it in the printer state that expects a refill action.",
	"SetAccessoriesUpgradeKit":       "Configure the firmware's upgrade-kit accessory record. Use this when implementing that hardware-setup workflow; the free-form fields are not validated as a known accessory model by this wrapper.",
	"ChamberModeSet":                 "Set the firmware's chamber mode flag. This is a chamber configuration operation, not a temperature target or fan percentage; its exact model-specific behavior is not established here.",
	"SetDoorStat":                    "Set the firmware's door-status configuration. Use this for supported door-related settings, not to physically open or close a door; the numeric config values are firmware-specific.",
	"GetDoorStat":                    "Ask the firmware for door-status information. Use this to inspect the supported door sensor/configuration workflow; it does not actuate the door.",
	"PrintCacheSet":                  "Enable or disable the firmware's print-cache setting. Use this to configure supported job caching behavior; this does not upload a file or start a print.",
	"PrintCacheGet":                  "Ask the firmware for its print-cache setting. Use this to inspect caching configuration before changing it.",
	"Sntp":                           "Set the printer clock using the app's time-synchronization fields. This supports timestamp/timezone synchronization; most integrations should leave clock management to the normal device workflow.",
	"GetAccessCode":                  "Request the printer's LAN access code. This is a credential-retrieval operation for trusted setup workflows, not a normal status query. Treat its response as a secret and do not expose it in public logs or dashboards.",
	"PushingStart":                   "Ask the printer to start pushing reports. This is an advanced report-stream control; normal integrations should use the events endpoint, which handles subscription without needing to reproduce Handy's stream setup.",
	"PushingStop":                    "Ask the printer to stop pushing reports. This is a protocol-level reporting control, not a print stop, and may affect status consumers; closing your events connection is the normal way to stop watching.",
	"PushAll":                        "Request a full printer-state report. Use this to resynchronize after incremental updates or a stale snapshot; a state read with refresh=1 already requests it for you.",
	"UploadStart":                    "Request a diagnostic-log upload for a time range. This exists for troubleshooting/support workflows and can transfer device data; use it only when deliberately collecting diagnostics for the supplied account/trouble identifiers.",
	"UploadGetInfo":                  "Ask for diagnostic-upload information. Use this to inspect the app's log-upload workflow rather than uploading new logs or querying print progress.",
	"AppCertList":                    "Query the printer's installed app certificates. This is one step of Bambu's secure-channel handshake, exposed for protocol diagnostics. Most integrations should use GET /v1/printers/{sn}/security for status or POST /v1/printers/{sn}/security/exchange for the managed handshake instead.",
	"AppCertInstall":                 "Install an app certificate on the printer for Bambu's signed command channel. This low-level handshake step exists because the wrapper exposes Handy's complete protocol, not because an ordinary application should install certificates itself. Prefer POST /v1/printers/{sn}/security/exchange: the wrapper obtains its app certificate, queries the printer and performs the exchange. It also attempts that exchange automatically after recognized signature-verification errors. Direct installation changes the printer's certificate state and has not been verified on real hardware from this repository; use it only for deliberate protocol work.",
	"CloseAirFilt":                   "Send the firmware's air-filter close action. This is a specialized airflow/filter workflow with model-specific effects; it is not a general fan speed control, so use fans/chamber for an ordinary speed adjustment.",
}

var routeDescriptions = map[string]string{
	"GET /v1/healthz":                         "Check whether this API process is running. Use this for a load balancer or uptime probe; a successful response does not prove that Bambu's cloud or a printer is reachable.",
	"GET /v1/catalog/endpoints":               "Inspect the reverse-engineered cloud endpoint catalog. Use this to discover upstream paths and parameters when implementing an advanced cloud integration, not for routine printer control.",
	"GET /v1/catalog/commands":                "Inspect every catalogued MQTT command and its wire parameters. This is for protocol tooling and low-level integrations; the resource-oriented printer routes are easier for normal applications.",
	"GET /v1/catalog/region":                  "Read this server's configured Bambu region and service hosts. Use this to diagnose account-region or cloud-connectivity mismatches; it does not switch the region.",
	"GET /v1/auth/pubkey":                     "Fetch Bambu's password-encryption public key. This is exposed for clients reproducing the login protocol; normal clients can call auth/login because the wrapper performs password encryption for them.",
	"POST /v1/auth/login":                     "Sign in to Bambu and, on a hosted server, store the resulting account session for API keys to use. Start here for password/email-code login; a verification-code or two-factor response means another step is required, not that login succeeded.",
	"POST /v1/auth/tfa":                       "Complete a login that returned a two-factor challenge. Supply the tfaKey from that login and the user's authenticator code; this is not an independent passwordless login route.",
	"POST /v1/auth/email-code":                "Send the email verification code required by the login workflow. Use this when login asks for code verification; it sends a real email and should not be called on every ordinary API request.",
	"GET /v1/me":                              "Read the authenticated account's Bambu cloud profile. Use this to show who is signed in or verify that credentials can reach the cloud; account returns the wrapper's stored-session information instead.",
	"GET /v1/keys":                            "List the API keys belonging to the signed-in account without returning their secrets. Use this to build a key-management screen; a Bambu login token, not an API key, is required.",
	"DELETE /v1/keys/{id}":                    "Revoke one account API key. Use this when an integration is retired or a key may be compromised; the key immediately stops authenticating, and open event streams end on their next credential recheck.",
	"GET /v1/devices":                         "List printers bound to the authenticated Bambu cloud account using the original upstream response shape. This exists for compatibility and low-level callers; new integrations should use GET /v1/printers for simpler fields without LAN access codes.",
	"GET /v1/devices/{sn}/state":              "Read the merged MQTT report snapshot for a printer. Use this for status displays, temperatures, job progress and AMS information without decoding individual reports. refresh=1 requests a fresh full report; otherwise previously received state may be returned.",
	"GET /v1/devices/{sn}/events":             "Watch the printer's MQTT reports as server-sent events. Use this for live dashboards instead of polling; the stream starts with cached state when available, then delivers incremental reports that callers must merge as needed.",
	"POST /v1/devices/{sn}/raw":               "Send an arbitrary MQTT module/command envelope. This escape hatch preserves access to undocumented or newer firmware commands; the wrapper cannot verify their meaning or safety, so prefer named resource routes whenever possible.",
	"POST /v1/devices/{sn}/gcode":             "Run raw G-code through the printer command channel. This exists for advanced operations without a dedicated endpoint and can cause real motion or heating; named controls are safer to integrate.",
	"POST /v1/devices/{sn}/move":              "Jog printer axes by relative distances in millimetres. This compatibility route uses the app's movement encoding; use it only with adequate clearance and an appropriate printer state.",
	"POST /v1/devices/{sn}/home":              "Home the selected axes, or all axes when the body is empty. This moves the printer to establish reference positions; it is a physical action rather than a position/status query.",
	"GET /v1/devices/{sn}/security":           "Inspect whether this wrapper currently has a secure channel established for the printer. Use this for authentication/signature troubleshooting, not to obtain an API key or cloud login session.",
	"DELETE /v1/devices/{sn}/security":        "Forget this wrapper's cached secure channel for the printer. Use this to reset local handshake state while debugging; it does not uninstall certificates on the printer or revoke account API keys.",
	"POST /v1/devices/{sn}/security/exchange": "Establish Bambu's signed command channel using the wrapper's managed certificate exchange. Use this when deliberately initializing or troubleshooting a printer that requires signed commands; normal commands also attempt the exchange automatically after recognized signature-verification errors. The wrapper fetches its app certificate and performs the handshake, so clients should not install certificates themselves. This workflow has been tested with a simulated printer, not real hardware from this repository.",
	"GET /v1/appcert":                         "Inspect the app certificate this wrapper uses for Bambu-signed cloud calls and printer handshakes. Use this for certificate-expiry or signing diagnostics; it returns public certificate metadata, not the private key, and may fetch the certificate on first use.",
	"GET /v1/lan/devices":                     "List printers registered in this single-user server's LAN registry. Use this to find local-only printers; the hosted multi-account server intentionally does not expose this global registry.",
	"POST /v1/lan/devices":                    "Register and connect to a LAN printer using its address, serial and access code. Use this instead of cloud account binding for local-only control; the registration is server-local and available only in single-user mode.",
	"DELETE /v1/lan/devices/{sn}":             "Remove a printer from this server's LAN registry and close its connection. This does not factory-reset the printer or unbind it from its Bambu cloud account.",
}
